# Line endings, pinned per file type - never left to core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: every shell and SQL file in this repo is EXECUTED inside a
# Linux container (`/bin/sh` for the maintenance jobs, the `sqlite3` CLI for the
# reconcile query). With a Windows checkout and `core.autocrlf=true` those files
# land with CRLF endings, and `/bin/sh` dies on the first line with
#   set: line 86: illegal option -
# BEFORE any job runs - so the nightly retention sweep and the Gate 2 money check
# never execute, while `docker compose config` still looks perfectly valid.
#
# FORCING LF IS THE FIX, NOT A PREFERENCE. These are not text files to be edited
# on Windows; they are programs the container runs.

# Shell scripts: runnable inside the container. LF is mandatory.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf

# SQL: applied by the sqlite3 CLI. A trailing CR becomes part of the statement.
*.sql           text eol=lf

# Container files: Dockerfiles, compose, nginx config. The image is Linux.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf

# TOML: baked into the server image by `COPY config` (server/Dockerfile:147), so it is a
# CONTAINER INPUT even though the server reads it with a TOML parser rather than a shell.
# It was missing from this list while shipping CRLF - measured inside a built image,
# /srv/apikita/config/apikita.toml had 637 CRLF lines.
#
# NOTHING BREAKS TODAY, and that is exactly why it belongs here. The failure this file
# exists to prevent is SILENT: the config inside the image is byte-different from what the
# repository stores, and the next consumer need not be a tolerant parser. A checksum, a
# byte-comparing test or a build-cache key would all see a difference nobody intended, on
# the machine that built it. The repository side is already correct - git stores LF and
# `core.autocrlf` adds the CR to the WORKING TREE - so this pins the checkout, not history.
*.toml          text eol=lf

# NOT added, and the omission is deliberate rather than an oversight: `*.ts` and `*.astro`.
# They are Linux-built in CI but were MEASURED to tolerate CRLF - 222 CRLF lines in a
# .astro page while all 163 website tests pass and the build succeeds - so adding them
# would be a guess, and this file should carry claims that were tested. Add them with
# evidence if a tool ever objects.

# Never text-normalise binaries or lock files: git would corrupt them.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.svg           text eol=lf
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.lock          binary
*.db            binary
*.zst           binary

# Generated artifacts are not source; leave their bytes alone.
website/dist/** binary
server/target/** binary